+    // NB: Both of these start worker threads (OpenSSL's thread pool, and SDL's libusb-based gamepad hotplug
+    //     detection thread), and Landlock does not confine a thread that already runs -- so both have to happen
+    //     after the sandbox is applied (see Compositor's restrict_filesystem(), which moved GPU driver init for the
+    //     same reason). SDL_Init failing (e.g. because Landlock didn't allow some USB/input device path it wants)
+    //     is treated as "no gamepad support" rather than fatal -- unlike the GPU backend, nothing else depends on it.
+    OPENSSL_TRY(OSSL_set_max_threads(nullptr, Core::System::hardware_concurrency()));
+
+    // NB: SDL's HIDAPI backend starts a libusb event-handling thread, which then needs seccomp syscalls (e.g.
+    //     sendmmsg on a netlink socket) that WebContent's policy doesn't grant. SDL_HINT_HIDAPI_LIBUSB=0 did NOT
+    //     avoid this in testing (hid_init() still calls libusb_init() regardless -- not fully root-caused). Rather
+    //     than widen the seccomp policy for a feature most pages never use, skip gamepad support entirely while
+    //     sandboxed; --disable-sandbox still gets it. Revisit if/when the actual libusb call site is found and a
+    //     narrower seccomp allowance (not blanket allow_network()) can be scoped to just what it needs.
+    if (disable_sandbox) {
+        if (!SDL_Init(SDL_INIT_GAMEPAD))
